WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.803
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B two.

2
00:00:06.103 --> 00:00:09.431
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:16.784
Unit thirty-nine: Review: B2 Grammar. Today you will review the main B2 grammar structures.

4
00:00:17.084 --> 00:00:19.643
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.007
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:26.060
Conditionals.

7
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.700
Conditionals means if sentences of every type.

8
00:00:30.283 --> 00:00:32.051
Second, third and mixed.

9
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.082
Your turn. Say: Conditionals.

10
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:39.062
Conditionals.

11
00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:40.678
Passive.

12
00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:44.287
Passive means be + past participle.

13
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:47.011
It was built in 1900.

14
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:50.362
Your turn. Say: Passive.

15
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.097
Passive.

16
00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:55.863
Deduction.

17
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.674
Deduction means conclusions with must and can't.

18
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:01.319
He must be home.

19
00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:04.917
Your turn. Say: Deduction.

20
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:08.698
Deduction.

21
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.405
Gerunds.

22
00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:13.915
Gerunds means -ing forms used as nouns.

23
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:15.785
I enjoy reading.

24
00:01:16.383 --> 00:01:19.430
Your turn. Say: Gerunds.

25
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:23.303
Gerunds.

26
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.318
Infinitives.

27
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:28.606
Infinitives means to + base verb.

28
00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:30.415
I want to learn.

29
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:33.530
Your turn. Say: Infinitives.

30
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:37.565
Infinitives.

31
00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:39.794
Relative clauses.

32
00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:44.164
Relative clauses means clauses with who, which, whose.

33
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.146
The man whose car...

34
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.971
Your turn. Say: Relative clauses.

35
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:54.264
Relative clauses.

36
00:01:55.017 --> 00:01:56.227
Future perfect.

37
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:00.226
Future perfect means will have + past participle.

38
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:02.584
By then I will have finished.

39
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:06.381
Your turn. Say: Future perfect.

40
00:02:09.233 --> 00:02:10.513
Future perfect.

41
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.239
Connectors.

42
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.598
Connectors means words linking ideas.

43
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:17.869
Furthermore, nevertheless.

44
00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:21.101
Your turn. Say: Connectors.

45
00:02:23.967 --> 00:02:24.933
Connectors.

46
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:29.210
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.501
Visal, which B2 structure do you use most confidently?

48
00:02:34.617 --> 00:02:46.836
I feel most confident with conditionals and connectors. B2 grammar lets you speak about unreal situations, make deductions, and write clear arguments with cohesion.

49
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.404
What are the main areas?

50
00:02:50.517 --> 00:03:07.182
Second, third and mixed conditionals, advanced passives, modals of deduction, gerunds and infinitives, relative clauses, future perfect, and discourse connectors. Inversion and cleft sentences add style.

51
00:03:08.283 --> 00:03:09.749
How should I continue?

52
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:24.314
Notice the structures when you read, then use them in your own writing. Review mistakes, and practise one structure at a time. Next comes C1, where precision, style and nuance matter even more.

53
00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:27.176
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:28.017 --> 00:03:30.701
Grammar focus: B2 grammar review.

55
00:03:31.001 --> 00:03:32.293
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:37.305
Conditional if + past perfect, would have If I had known, I would have gone.

57
00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:40.924
Passive is said to be He is said to be rich.

58
00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:43.863
Connector nevertheless, we continued.

59
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:54.082
Review conditionals, advanced passives, deduction, gerunds / infinitives, relative clauses, future perfect and connectors. Use them in your own writing.

60
00:03:54.817 --> 00:03:58.514
Be careful! Don't say: If I would know, I'd tell you.

61
00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:02.791
Say: If I knew, I would tell you.

62
00:04:03.091 --> 00:04:04.534
Use the past simple.

63
00:04:05.333 --> 00:04:07.928
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:10.036
B2 in Review.

65
00:04:10.336 --> 00:04:14.902
B2 grammar helps you discuss unreal situations and make arguments.

66
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.002
Conditionals, passives and modal deduction add precision.

67
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.114
Relative clauses and connectors make writing cohesive.

68
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.854
Formal structures such as inversion add style.

69
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:30.888
Next comes C1, where nuance matters even more.

70
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.839
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:36.683 --> 00:04:38.590
If I had known, I blank gone.

72
00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:42.416
A, would have. B, will have. C, would.

73
00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:47.711
The answer is A: would have.

74
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.105
He blank be at home; the lights are on.

75
00:04:51.405 --> 00:04:54.664
A, can't. B, mustn't. C, must.

76
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.025
The answer is C: must.

77
00:05:01.183 --> 00:05:03.473
By next year I blank finished my degree.

78
00:05:03.773 --> 00:05:07.148
A, will have. B, will be. C, have.

79
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.885
The answer is A: will have.

80
00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.189
Which connector shows contrast?

81
00:05:16.489 --> 00:05:20.595
A, moreover. B, consequently. C, nevertheless.

82
00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:26.620
The answer is C: nevertheless.

83
00:05:27.783 --> 00:05:30.398
According to the reading, what comes next?

84
00:05:30.698 --> 00:05:34.468
A, C1. B, A1. C, B1.

85
00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:40.230
The answer is A: C1.

86
00:05:41.433 --> 00:05:44.736
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:48.465
Which B2 structure do you use most confidently?

88
00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:58.785
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:59.085 --> 00:06:11.910
I use conditionals and connectors most confidently. They help me explain unreal situations and organise my ideas clearly. I still want to practise inversion and cleft sentences.

90
00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.897
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:14.783 --> 00:06:19.003
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and b2 grammar review.

92
00:06:19.303 --> 00:06:23.697
Today's value is lifelong learning. Keep learning for as long as you live.

93
00:06:23.997 --> 00:06:30.704
Congratulations! You have finished Online Grammar, level B two. See you in Online Grammar, level C one!

94
00:06:31.004 --> 00:06:34.387
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

